Certainly! Here's a simple example of a blog post layout using HTML and CSS that focuses purely on informational content:

```html
<article class="blog-post">
 <header class="post-header">
 <h1 class="post-title">Exploring the Basics of HTML5</h1>
 <div class="post-meta">
 <span class="author">Written by Alex Johnson</span>
 <time datetime="2023-10-12">October 12, 2023</time>
 </div>
 </header>
 <section class="post-content">
 <p>HTML5 is the latest evolution of the standard that defines HTML. It represents two different concepts:</p>
 <ul>
 <li>A new version of the language HTML, with new elements, attributes, and behaviors.</li>
 <li>A larger set of technologies that allows for more diverse and powerful websites and applications.</li>
 </ul>
 <p>Among its features are the support for canvas elements for drawing, native support for audio and video embedding, and more semantic document structuring elements like <code>&lt;article&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;section&gt;</code>.</p>
 </section>
 <footer class="post-footer">
 <div class="tags">
 <span class="tag">#HTML5</span>
 <span class="tag">#WebDevelopment</span>
 </div>
 <div class="comments">
 <h2>Comments</h2>
 <p>No comments yet. Be the first to comment!</p>
 </div>
 </footer>
</article>
```

```css
.blog-post {
 font-family: 'Arial', sans-serif;
 max-width: 800px;
 margin: 0 auto;
 padding: 20px;
 border: 1px solid #e0e0e0;
 box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
}
.post-header {
 border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd;
 margin-bottom: 20px;
}
.post-title {
 font-size: 2em;
 margin: 0 0 10px;
}
.post-meta {
 font-size: 0.9em;
 color: #555;
}
.post-content {
 line-height: 1.6;
}
.tag {
 display: inline-block;
 background-color: #f3f3f3;
 padding: 5px;
 margin-right: 5px;
 border-radius: 3px;
}
.comments {
 margin-top: 20px;
}
```

